 Quantum computing has been demonstrably proven to be superior than classical computing when solving problems such as searching an unstructured database  #ref10# or factorization of large numbers  #ref22#. Due to the rising interest in the computational capabilities of noisy intermediate scale quantum (NISQ) computers and the availability of experimentation platforms, there has been a rapid growth in the development of circuit based algorithms. Variational Quantum Circuits or Parametrized Quantum circuits are one such class of the circuit based algorithms that has been studied extensively  #ref1#, specifically in context  of Quantum Machine Learning (QML) \cite{arxivID-2108.09664-0, arxivID-2005.04316-1, arxivID-2005.04316-2, arxivID-2005.04316-3}. VQCs have been used to replace neural networks in various machine learning tasks, such as classification \cite{arxivID-2106.07327-4}, regression \cite{arxivID-1808.09607-5}, clustering \cite{arxivID-1908.06655-6}, reinforcement learning \cite{arxivID-2210.14876-7}, etc. Since the discovery of the potential of VQCs, several architectures have been proposed to solve these tasks. However, due to the challenges in scaling up quantum circuits, the number of qubits that can be used is limited, resulting in the circuits being limited in terms of the number of layers and the number of gates. This limits the expressibility of the circuits, making it difficult to implement complex tasks. To address this shortcoming, we propose a novel approach to compress the state within the quantum circuit using tensor ring representations. This allows for the use of more qubits, thereby increasing the expressibility of the quantum circuit. The main contributions of this paper are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
\item A novel algorithm for compressing the quantum state using tensor ring representation.
\item An approximation for two qubit gates, which allows for the use of more qubits and layers in the quantum circuit.
\item An algorithm for training the parameters of the tensor ring representation using gradient descent.
\item An algorithm for implementing tensor ring representation in VQCs.
\item Evaluation of the proposed approach on two datasets: Iris and MNIST for the classification task.
\end{itemize}

The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 presents the background and related work. Section 3 presents the proposed approach. Section 4 presents the experimental results, and Section 5 presents the conclusion and future work.
Related Work
\subsection{Tensor Networks}
Tensor Networks are a mathematical tool that is used to represent high dimensional tensors as a network of low dimensional tensors. This allows for efficient representation of high dimensional tensors, which can be used to solve problems that are otherwise intractable. Tensor Networks have been used in various fields such as quantum mechanics \cite{arxivID-1811.09833-8}, machine learning \cite{arxivID-1605.05775-9}, and condensed matter physics \cite{arxivID-2104.05439-10}

