 As a fine-grained classification problem, person re-identification (ReID) aims at identifying a specific person across non-overlapping camera views. Existing ReID methods have achieved a remarkable success in both supervised  #ref41# #ref36# #ref34# #ref48# #ref45# #ref24# #ref26# and unsupervised #ref44# #ref29# #ref13# #ref14# #ref25# #ref10# domains. Most of these approaches directly leverage the weights pre-trained on ImageNet for model initialization, which is  a common practice in many computer vision tasks. However, due to the large domain gap between the ImageNet classification task and the person ReID task, the pre-trained weights may not be optimal for ReID. Therefore, how to pre-train a robust and discriminative ReID model is still a challenging problem.

Recently, self-supervised contrastive learning (CL) has achieved great success in learning high-quality representations in many vision tasks \cite{arxivID-2011.00362-0, arxivID-2305.13689-1}. However, it is non-trivial to directly apply the CL framework to the ReID task. The main reason is that the contrastive loss used in CL is designed to learn a general representation for image classification, while the ReID task is a fine-grained classification task that requires more discriminative and robust features. Besides, the contrastive loss in CL is typically defined between global image-level features, while the ReID task requires local feature learning to capture the discriminative clues of the person images. Therefore, there are two critical issues in the application of CL to the ReID task: (1) the augmentations in CL pipeline may distort the discriminative clues in person images, and (2) the fine-grained local features of person images are not fully-explored.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{figures/fig1.pdf}
\caption{The performance comparison of the ReID models pre-trained on ImageNet and our UP-ReID on the Market1501 dataset.}
\label{fig1}
\end{figure}

To address the above two issues, in this paper, we propose an Unsupervised Pre-training framework for ReID based on the contrastive learning (CL) pipeline, dubbed UP-ReID. The main idea of UP-ReID is to introduce an intra-identity regularization in the CL pipeline to learn robust and discriminative ReID features. Specifically, we instantiate the intra-identity regularization as two constraints: (1) a global consistency is enforced between the augmented and original person images to increase robustness to augmentation, and (2) an intrinsic contrastive constraint among the local patches of each image is employed to fully explore the local discriminative clues. By introducing these two constraints, the UP-ReID framework can learn more robust and discriminative features for the ReID task. Extensive experiments on multiple popular Re-ID datasets, including PersonX, Market1501, CUHK03, and MSMT17, demonstrate that our UP-ReID pre-trained model can significantly benefit the downstream ReID fine-tuning and achieve state-of-the-art performance.

In summary, the main contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose an Unsupervised Pre-training framework for ReID based on the contrastive learning (CL) pipeline, dubbed UP-ReID, which can learn more robust and discriminative ReID features.
\item We instantiate the intra-identity regularization as two constraints: a global consistency is enforced between the augmented and original person images to increase robustness to augmentation, and an intrinsic contrastive constraint among the local patches of each image is employed to fully explore the local discriminative clues.
\item Extensive experiments on multiple popular Re-ID datasets demonstrate that our UP-ReID pre-trained model can significantly benefit the downstream ReID fine-tuning and achieve state-of-the-art performance.
\end{itemize}

