 The technical challenge of scaling permissionless blockchains has been a hot research topic for the last few years. With various scaling solutions, be it Ethereum 2.0s sharding #ref25# or Confluxs Tree-Graph ledger structure #ref20#, the consensus mechanism ceases to be the performance bottleneck. While disk I/O, network bandwidth, and transaction execution are all possible sources  of performance issues, transaction execution is the most critical factor. In a recent study \cite{arxivID-2003.06128-0}, the authors studied the performance of the transaction execution layer in the Ethereum system. They found that there is significant potential for parallel execution of transactions. However, their proposed parallelization scheme cannot effectively deal with the conflicts inherent in many blockchain applications, and the reported speedup is far from ideal. In this work, we propose a parallel execution scheme that can exploit the concurrency in the transaction execution layer more effectively. Our scheme is based on the observation that the conflicts in many blockchain applications are application-inherent. In other words, the conflicts in these applications cannot be resolved by existing concurrency control mechanisms. We propose a novel approach to resolve these conflicts by using partitioned counters and special commutative instructions. This approach allows us to break up the application conflict chains and to maximize the potential speedup. We also propose OCC-DA, an optimistic concurrency control scheduler with deterministic aborts, which makes it possible to use OCC scheduling in public blockchain settings. When we evaluated the maximum parallel speedup achievable, these techniques doubled this limit to an 18x overall speedup compared to serial execution, thus approaching the optimum. Our main contributions are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
\item We thoroughly study the historical transaction execution traces in Ethereum. We observe that application-inherent conflicts are the major factors that limit the exploitable parallelism during execution.
\item We propose to use partitioned counters and special commutative instructions to break up the application conflict chains in order to maximize the potential speedup.
\item We propose OCC-DA, an optimistic concurrency control scheduler with deterministic aborts, which makes it possible to use OCC scheduling in public blockchain settings.
\end{itemize}

The rest of this paper is organized as follows. We first give a brief overview of the Ethereum system in Section 2. In Section 3, we present the details of our parallel execution scheme. In Section 4, we evaluate the performance of our scheme. Finally, we discuss the related work in Section 5 and conclude in Section 6.
