In this section, we discuss the related work on improving the performance of blockchain systems, and we also discuss some existing concurrency control mechanisms that can be used in blockchains.

\subsection{Improving Blockchain Performance}

There are many works that aim to improve the performance of blockchain systems. Some of these works focus on improving the performance of the consensus protocol, while others focus on improving the performance of the execution layer.

\subsubsection{Improving Consensus Protocol Performance}

One way to improve the performance of the consensus protocol is to use sharding. Sharding divides the network into smaller committees, which reduces the size of the network that needs to reach consensus, and thus reduces the time it takes to reach consensus. For example, Ethereum 2.0 uses sharding to improve the performance of the consensus protocol. In Ethereum 2.0, the network is divided into six committees, each of which has 32 validators. These committees run the consensus protocol independently from each other, and the results of the consensus protocol are combined at the end. This reduces the time it takes to reach consensus, because each committee only needs to reach consensus with the other committees in the same shard.

Another way to improve the performance of the consensus protocol is to use a different consensus protocol altogether. For example, the Algorand protocol \cite{arxivID-2104.15063-1} uses a randomized consensus protocol that is designed to be fast and secure. The protocol is based on a random committee of nodes that is elected to reach consensus. The random committee is chosen in such a way that it is unlikely that any one node in the committee has enough power to influence the outcome of the consensus protocol. This makes it difficult for an attacker to influence the outcome of the consensus protocol.

\subsubsection{Improving Execution Layer Performance}

Another way to improve the performance of the blockchain system is to improve the performance of the execution layer. The execution layer is the part of the blockchain system that processes the transactions and updates the state of the blockchain. There are many ways to improve the performance of the execution layer, including using more efficient data structures, optimizing the execution of smart contracts, and using parallel execution.

One way to improve the performance of the execution layer is to use more efficient data structures. For example, some blockchain systems use a trie data structure to store the state of the blockchain. A trie is a tree-like data structure that is used to store data in a way that allows for efficient retrieval. By using a trie, the blockchain system can retrieve data more quickly than if it were stored in a traditional data structure, such as a hash table.

Another way to improve the performance of the execution layer is to optimize the execution of smart contracts. Smart contracts are programs that are executed on the blockchain. By optimizing the execution of smart contracts, the blockchain system can reduce the amount of time it takes to execute transactions. There are many ways to optimize the execution of smart contracts, including using more efficient algorithms, using more efficient data structures, and using parallel execution.

