 Chest X-ray (CXR) imaging is used as a first-line test for identifying lung anomalies because it provides fast image generation with a low radiation dose  #ref1#. However, it is difficult to accurately diagnose diseases under fine shading conditions. Existing systems and methods should be further improved as precise  and stable abnormal region localization (AL-CXR) is required for clinical applications.

In recent years, deep learning-based AL-CXR has been actively studied to overcome the limitations of conventional methods, such as the high cost of manual annotation. The AL-CXR task is a binary classification problem for identifying the presence or absence of abnormal regions in CXR images. However, it is difficult to accurately localize abnormal regions without pixel-level annotation. Therefore, most methods based on deep learning have focused on weakly supervised learning, such as image-level annotation \cite{arxivID-1807.01257-0, arxivID-1906.09354-1, arxivID-2004.11693-2, arxivID-2004.11693-3, arxivID-2004.11693-4, arxivID-2004.11693-5}. However, these methods have limitations in terms of the accuracy of AL-CXR, as they are based on weakly supervised learning.

