 Generative adversarial networks (GANs) are generative models used for image synthesis in the computer vision domain  #ref1#. Image synthesis refers to the generation of new synthetic images replicating the feature distribution of training images. GANs have demonstrated remarkable advancements in image synthesis in several domains such as  facial images \cite{arxivID-1811.00112-0} synthesize faces of existing subjects with different poses and expressions, they are not well suited for increasing the number of subjects. In contrast, our approach uses a conditional GAN to synthesize faces of new subjects. This allows us to effectively increase the number of subjects in a face dataset without having to manually collect additional images, which is very time-consuming and expensive. The proposed method is also capable of generating faces with different poses and expressions.

\subsection{Motivation}

X-ray images are widely used in the medical domain to detect diseases and injuries. However, the availability of X-ray images is limited due to the privacy and safety concerns of patients. To address this problem, GANs are utilized to generate synthetic X-ray images that can be used to train machine learning models. However, training GANs is challenging as they are prone to mode collapse, non-convergence, and instability, which can result in low-quality synthetic images. To address these challenges, we propose a novel GAN architecture that incorporates attention mechanism and multi-scale gradient learning to improve the training process and generate high-quality synthetic X-ray images.

\subsection{Research Objectives}

The objective of this work is to propose a novel GAN architecture that incorporates attention mechanism and multi-scale gradient learning to improve the training process and generate high-quality synthetic X-ray images. The proposed architecture will be evaluated on a publicly available X-ray dataset to demonstrate its effectiveness in generating diverse and high-quality synthetic images.

\subsection{Research Questions}

The following research questions will guide the study:
\begin{itemize}
\item How can attention mechanism and multi-scale gradient learning be incorporated into a GAN architecture to improve the training process and generate high-quality synthetic X-ray images?
\item How effective is the proposed architecture in generating diverse and high-quality synthetic images compared to existing GAN architectures?
\item How can the proposed architecture be evaluated on a publicly available X-ray dataset to demonstrate its effectiveness in generating diverse and high-quality synthetic images?
