In this paper, we propose S5CL, a unified framework for fully-supervised, self-supervised, and semi-supervised learning. S5CL combines these three learning paradigms into one training stage by introducing three contrastive losses: a supervised loss for labeled images, a self-supervised loss for unlabeled images, and a semi-supervised loss for pseudo-labeled images (see Fig.~\ref{fig:s5cl}). These losses are designed to learn feature representations that reflect the hierarchy of distance relationships: similar images and augmentations are embedded the closest, followed by different looking images of the same class, while images from separate classes have the largest distance. Moreover, S5CL allows us to flexibly combine these losses to adapt to different scenarios. Evaluations of our framework on two public histopathological datasets show strong improvements in the case of sparse labels: for a H\#eq0\#E-stained colorectal cancer dataset, the accuracy increases by up to \eq{1} compared to supervised cross-entropy loss; for a highly imbalanced dataset of single white blood cells from leukemia patient blood smears, the F1-score increases by up to \eq{2}.111

\textbf{Contributions:} Our contributions are three-fold: (1) We propose S5CL, a unified framework for fully-supervised, self-supervised, and semi-supervised learning; (2) We introduce three contrastive losses that allow us to learn feature representations that reflect the hierarchy of distance relationships; (3) We show strong improvements in the case of sparse labels on two public histopathological datasets. <|section_end|>

