        In the last years, several datasets have been introduced for FER. In the following, we describe the most relevant ones.

        The FER2013 \cite{arxivID-2006.04057-8} dataset contains 35,887 images of faces labeled with one of seven emotions: anger, disgust, fear, happiness, sadness, surprise, and neutral. The dataset is split into training, validation and test sets with 28,709, 3,589 and 3,589 images, respectively. The images were collected from the internet and are not in a controlled environment. The images are of different sizes and have different resolutions. The FER2013 dataset is the most widely used dataset for FER, but it has some limitations. The dataset is small and the images are not in a controlled environment, so the faces are not well aligned and the background is not clean.

        The CK+ dataset \cite{arxivID-1306.1913-9} contains 320 images of seven different emotions (anger, disgust, fear, happiness, sadness, surprise, and neutral) captured from 12 actors. The images were captured in a controlled environment and the faces are well aligned. However, the dataset is small and the emotions are not very diverse.

        The RAF-DB dataset \cite{arxivID-2008.05196-10} contains 24,702 images of faces labeled with one of seven emotions: anger, disgust, fear, happiness, sadness, surprise, and neutral. The dataset is split into training, validation and test sets with 16,802, 4,802 and 3,100 images, respectively. The images were collected from the internet and are not in a controlled environment. The images are of different sizes and have different resolutions. The RAF-DB dataset is larger than FER2013 and the images are in a controlled environment, so the faces are well aligned and the background is clean.

        The AffectNet dataset \cite{arxivID-1708.03985-11} contains 1,000,000 images of faces labeled with one of seven emotions: anger, disgust, fear, happiness, sadness, surprise, and neutral. The dataset is split into training, validation and test sets with 700,000, 150,000 and 150,000 images, respectively. The images were collected from the internet and are not in a controlled environment. The images are of different sizes and have different resolutions. The AffectNet dataset is the largest dataset for FER, but the images are not in a controlled environment, so the faces are not well aligned and the background is not clean.

