\subsection{Related Work}
There is a plethora of works in the literature that have been published on the subject of place recognition and re-localization, however, most of them focus on visual data. In the case of LiDAR data, most of the methods are based on scan matching and therefore, are vulnerable in featureless environments like caves and tunnels, as well as in environments with repetitive structures, like industrial environments \cite{arxivID-1212.1313-0, arxivID-2212.14209-1, arxivID-2103.03626-2}.

In \cite{arxivID-2103.09605-3}, the authors use intensity information for place recognition, while in \cite{arxivID-1909.07267-4}, the authors use LiDAR descriptors for place recognition. In \cite{arxivID-1706.08775-5}, the authors combine visual odometry with topological localization, while in \cite{arxivID-2006.05137-6}, the authors propose a method for precise localization in inaccurate building models. In \cite{arxivID-2209.01605-7}, the authors propose a method for accurate localization in a pre-built LiDAR point cloud. In \cite{arxivID-2308.05593-8}, the authors address the problem of robust localization in cluttered indoor environments. In \cite{arxivID-1910.12728-9}, the authors propose a method for long-term localization in a changing environment. In \cite{arxivID-1910.03336-10}, the authors propose a method for improving map re-localization with deep learning. In \cite{arxivID-2304.08660-11}, the authors propose a method for LiDAR localization without a prior point cloud map. In \cite{arxivID-2206.15154-12}, the authors propose a method for semantic place recognition and pose estimation from 3D LiDAR.

