The task of global re-localization in subterranean environments has been widely studied in the literature. One of the most common approaches is the use of descriptors and feature matching. However, in subterranean environments, the lack of features makes it difficult to use such methods. In this case, data-driven approaches can be used to learn features that are more robust to changes in the environment.

A common approach for global re-localization is to use descriptors to match the current scan with a database of previously mapped scans. In this case, the descriptors are used to extract features from the scans, and then the features are matched to find the best match. However, this approach is not robust to changes in the environment, such as changes in lighting conditions or changes in the position of the robot. To overcome these limitations, data-driven approaches can be used to learn features that are more robust to changes in the environment. One such approach is the use of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to extract features from the scans. CNNs have been shown to be effective at extracting features from images, and they can also be used to extract features from point clouds. Another approach is the use of deep learning to learn features directly from the scans. Deep learning has been shown to be effective at learning features from data, and it can be used to learn features that are more robust to changes in the environment. However, these methods require a lot of data to train the models, and they can be computationally expensive.

In this paper, we propose a novel global re-localization framework for subterranean environments, which uses a data-driven descriptor extraction framework for place recognition and orientation regression, and a junction detection module for classification purposes. The proposed method is able to provide the top-k candidates to choose from, and it does not require an initial guess, which makes it suitable for use in GPS-denied environments. The proposed method is also able to provide an event-based re-localization trigger module for enabling and supporting completely autonomous robotic missions.

