 Network virtualization (NV) is an emerging technology for the dynamic planning of Internet architecture  #ref1# #ref20#. By slicing the network, it can effectively overcome the rigidity of the network architecture. Specifically, by sharing infrastructure, NV enables multiple heterogeneous virtual networks (VNs) deploy in the  same physical network, thereby improving resource utilization. Virtual Network Embedding (VNE) is a fundamental problem of NV. It aims to map the resource demands of VNs onto a physical network while ensuring that the mapping satisfies all resource constraints. VNE is a combinatorial optimization problem that is NP-hard \cite{arxivID-1801.03162-0}. Its goal is to improve the scalability and flexibility of the network by decoupling the resources and services of the underlying physical network. VNE can be considered as a subproblem of the network virtualization problem, which aims to find a feasible mapping of the VNs to the physical network. VNE is also a critical problem for network resource management, which has received widespread attention in recent years. VNE can be divided into two categories: static VNE and dynamic VNE. Static VNE aims to find a feasible embedding of VNs to the physical network, while dynamic VNE aims to find a feasible embedding of VNs to the physical network in a dynamic network environment. The traditional VNE methods are based on heuristic algorithms, such as genetic algorithms, ant colony algorithms, and particle swarm optimization algorithms. However, these methods have limitations in terms of solution quality and time complexity. With the development of deep learning and reinforcement learning, some deep learning-based and reinforcement learning-based methods have been proposed for VNE. Deep learning-based methods can effectively solve the static VNE problem, while reinforcement learning-based methods can effectively solve the dynamic VNE problem. However, these methods have limitations in terms of model complexity and training time. With the development of distributed computing and federated learning, some distributed computing-based and federated learning-based methods have been proposed for VNE. Distributed computing-based methods can effectively solve the large-scale VNE problem, while federated learning-based methods can effectively solve the privacy-preserving VNE problem. However, these methods have limitations in terms of model accuracy and communication efficiency. In summary, the existing VNE methods have different advantages and disadvantages in terms of solution quality, time complexity, model complexity, training time, model accuracy, and communication efficiency. Therefore, it is necessary to continuously explore new methods for VNE to improve the efficiency and accuracy of VNE.

