 Path planning is a common task in robotics applications, but it can be hard to handle by standard tools of the classical optimal control theory when the system is subject to stochastic perturbations or adversarial interactions. It becomes even harder when some important aspects of the dynamics or the environment are not just uncertain, but completely unknown in advance. With repeated tasks, the Reinforcement Learning (RL) provides a popular framework for optimizing the system performance based on the information accumulated in  the course of the task execution. This paper considers a 2D continuous path planning problem with a completely unknown intensity of random termination: an Evader is trying to escape a domain while minimizing the cumulative risk of detection (termination) by adversarial Observers. Those Observers surveillance intensity is a priori unknown and has to be learned through repetitive path planning. We propose a new algorithm that utilizes Gaussian process regression to model the unknown surveillance intensity and relies on a confidence bound technique to promote strategic exploration. We illustrate our method through several examples and confirm the convergence of averaged regret experimentally.

\subsection{Related work}

In the literature, the path planning problem with known adversarial surveillance is commonly referred to as the \textit{surveillance-evasion game} \cite{arxivID-2305.19832-0}. The general problem is considered to be hard to solve \cite{arxivID-1602.01896-1}, but some special cases are solvable \cite{arxivID-2401.12848-2, arxivID-1505.00297-3}. In \cite{arxivID-1812.10620-4}, the authors consider a similar problem with finite set of possible surveillance plans and provide an algorithm to compute the Nash equilibrium. The problem of surveillance-evasion game with unknown adversarial surveillance is considered in \cite{arxivID-2109.14811-5}, where the authors introduce a Bayesian reinforcement learning algorithm that relies on a Gaussian Process regression, numerical methods for Hamilton-Jacobi PDEs, and Confidence Bounds.

The main challenge of solving the path planning problem is the dimensionality of the state space, which makes the standard discretization approaches computationally infeasible. This problem is usually addressed through the use of continuous approximations. For example, the authors of \cite{arxivID-2205.00628-6} use the path integral method to solve the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman PDE in a continuous-time continuous-space chance-constrained stochastic optimal control problem. The authors of \cite{arxivID-1510.05344-7} combine the path integral method with a receding-horizon scheme in execution of the optimal control solution to generate a dynamically feasible and collision-free trajectory. The authors of \cite{arxivID-1612.05594-8} propose a sampling-based planning and optimal control method of nonlinear systems under non-differentiable constraints. The authors of \cite{arxivID-1702.07335-9} provide an efficient algorithm that yields approximately optimal policies with arbitrary higher-order nonlinear performance indices. The authors of \cite{arxivID-2210.02131-10} use a neural network and the Liouville equation to learn the density evolution for a system with an uncertain initial state. The authors of \cite{arxivID-2403.14956-11} propose a boundary-aware safe stochastic planning framework that generates a value function that can strictly distinguish the state values between free (safe) and non-navigable (boundary) spaces in the continuous state. The authors of \cite{arxivID-1903.00948-12} propose a solution to the decision-theoretic planning problem that directly obtains the continuous value function with only the first and second moments of the transition probabilities.

\subsection{Our contribution}
In this paper, we introduce a new algorithm that utilizes Gaussian process regression to model the unknown surveillance intensity and relies on a confidence bound technique to promote strategic exploration. We illustrate our method through several examples and confirm the convergence of averaged regret experimentally. <|section_end|>

