The problem of path planning has been widely studied in the literature, for instance in \cite{arxivID-2208.12135-0}. In this work, the path is discretised to obtain an approximate numerical solution, but without requiring the signed curvature to be monotone. Paths with strictly-monotone signed curvature constitute an important class of curves. Clothoids are used for highway and railway track design, and in path-planning for autonomous vehicles, because they have linear signed curvature. This allows vehicles to transition smoothly between two paths. A method for generating feasible velocity profiles along clothoids is proposed in, but time-optimality is not claimed. Since the Cartesian parametric equations of a clothoid require the evaluation of Fresnel Integrals, they are often approximated with polynomial series expansions instead. The strict monotonicity assumption on path curvature here admits both clothoids and their polynomial approximations. Beyond these, any path of practical use can be decomposed into a finite number of segments with either strictly-monotonic or constant signed curvature. Since velocity profiling for the latter is trivial, and velocity profiling for the former is treated here, a time-optimal velocity profile for the entire path can be constructed by choosing the right boundary conditions for each segment. This reduces an infinite-dimensional optimisation problem to a finite-dimensional one, without introducing any approximations. Extensions in this direction are the subject of ongoing work. <|section_end|>

 Various empirical studies have shown that an important characteristic of neural networks is their feature learning ability, i.e., to learn a feature mapping for the inputs which allow accurate prediction (e.g., #ref55# #ref25# #ref56# #ref42#). This is widely believed to be a key factor to their remarkable success in many applications, in particular, an advantage over traditional machine learning methods. To understand their success, it is then crucial to understand  the source and benefit of feature learning.

To this end, we consider the following learning problem. The input data is generated from a distribution which contains class relevant patterns and some background patterns. The labels are determined by a set of class relevant patterns. The goal is to learn a classifier to predict the labels based on the inputs. This problem is motivated by practical data, where the inputs contain both class relevant patterns and some background patterns, and the labels are determined by the class relevant patterns.

We prove that neural networks trained by gradient descent can succeed on this problem, which means they can learn a classifier with small generalization errors. The success relies on the emergence and improvement of effective features, which are learned among exponentially many candidates efficiently by exploiting the data (in particular, the structure of the input distribution). In contrast, no linear models on data-independent features of polynomial sizes can learn to as good errors. Furthermore, if the specific input structure is removed, then no polynomial algorithm in the Statistical Query model can learn even weakly. These results provide theoretical evidence showing that feature learning in neural networks depends strongly on the input structure and leads to the superior performance. Our preliminary experimental results on synthetic and real data also provide positive support.

