A critical aspect of MRC is to evaluate the reasoning capabilities of a model. However, recent works \cite{arxivID-1808.04926-3, arxivID-2003.04642-4} have identified several biases in the current MRC datasets. These biases make it easier for a model to overfit by relying on spurious correlations or naive data patterns. A model can achieve high accuracy by relying on lexical cues without actually comprehending the text \cite{arxivID-1808.08744-5, arxivID-1910.07350-6}. A model can also rely on a question answering pattern observed in the training set without comprehending the text. For example, a model can achieve high accuracy on the MovieQA dataset \cite{arxivID-1512.02902-7} by simply using the last sentence of the context as the answer. This is because the dataset is constructed by taking the last sentence of the context as the answer and asking a question about it. Thus, the models can achieve high accuracy by simply learning this pattern without actually comprehending the text.

Recently, \cite{arxivID-2110.11899-8} proposed a framework to address some of these biases. They propose a benchmark (referred to as Meta-RecipeQA) to evaluate the reasoning capabilities of a model. They also propose a hierarchical transformer based reasoning network (HTRN) that achieves state-of-the-art performance on the Meta-RecipeQA dataset. However, the Meta-RecipeQA benchmark is limited in terms of the number of questions and the number of control knobs. For example, there are only two control knobs (i.e. question type and answer type) and only three types of questions (i.e. entity, event, and temporal). The limited number of questions and control knobs makes it difficult to evaluate the reasoning capabilities of a model. Moreover, the Meta-RecipeQA benchmark is constructed using the same context as the RecipeQA dataset. As a result, the Meta-RecipeQA benchmark is not completely unbiased and may not be able to provide a fair evaluation of a model's reasoning capabilities.

In this paper, we propose a new benchmark for evaluating the reasoning capabilities of a model. Our benchmark is based on the Meta-RecipeQA dataset \cite{arxivID-2110.11899-9}. We propose a new framework for generating questions and answers that is more general and flexible than the framework proposed by \cite{arxivID-2110.11899-10}. Our framework allows us to generate a large number of questions and control knobs, which makes it possible to evaluate the reasoning capabilities of a model more accurately. We also propose a new hierarchical transformer based reasoning network (HTRN) that achieves state-of-the-art performance on the new benchmark. We show that our new benchmark is more challenging than the Meta-RecipeQA benchmark and that our new HTRN model is more accurate than the HTRN model proposed by \cite{arxivID-2110.11899-11}.

Our contributions in this paper are:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a new framework for generating questions and answers that is more general and flexible than the framework proposed by \cite{arxivID-2110.11899-12}.
\item We propose a new hierarchical transformer based reasoning network (HTRN) that achieves state-of-the-art performance on the new benchmark.
\item We show that our new benchmark is more challenging than the Meta-RecipeQA benchmark and that our new HTRN model is more accurate than the HTRN model proposed by \cite{arxivID-2110.11899-13}.
