While the RecipeQA dataset is a valuable contribution to the field, it has some limitations. First, the questions generated by the dataset are limited to a few templates and the answers are often extracted from the context without any additional reasoning. This can make it easier for a model to overfit by relying on spurious correlations or naive data patterns. Second, the dataset is relatively small, which may limit its ability to generalize to other domains or tasks. Third, the dataset does not provide a systematic evaluation of the model's performance across different tasks, which can make it difficult to compare different models and identify areas for improvement.

In this work, we propose a systematic framework to address these limitations by generating a test bed of datasets of progressive difficulty levels. We also propose a novel hierarchical transformer based reasoning network (HTRN) that can be used to address the limitations of the RecipeQA dataset. Our experimental results show that HTRN outperforms the previous state-of-the-art models on the RecipeQA dataset, demonstrating the effectiveness of our approach. We also provide a detailed analysis of the performance of our model on different tasks and datasets, which can help researchers better understand the strengths and weaknesses of different approaches. Finally, we discuss the potential impact of our work on the field of multimodal machine comprehension and suggest directions for future research.

