\subsection{Multimodal Machine Comprehension}
Multimodal machine comprehension (MMC) is an important research area that aims to develop models that can understand and reason about multimodal information, such as text and images. MMC has gained significant attention in recent years due to its potential applications in various domains, including question answering, visual dialog, and multimedia retrieval. The MMC task is typically formulated as a machine comprehension problem, where a model is given a multimodal context (e.g. an image and a caption) and a question, and is required to generate an answer. MMC is a challenging task that requires the model to integrate information from different modalities and perform complex reasoning and inference. In this paper, we propose a novel hierarchical transformer-based reasoning network (HTRN) for MMC and evaluate its performance on the RecipeQA dataset. Our results demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach and its potential for advancing the state-of-the-art in MMC.

\subsection{RecipeQA Dataset}
The RecipeQA dataset is a multimodal machine comprehension dataset that consists of cooking recipes and corresponding questions and answers. The dataset is based on the Recipe1M+ dataset, which contains over 1 million cooking recipes. The RecipeQA dataset is publicly available and can be used for research purposes. The RecipeQA dataset has been widely used in the literature for evaluating MMC models and has been shown to be a challenging dataset that requires models to integrate information from different modalities and perform complex reasoning and inference. In this paper, we use the RecipeQA dataset to evaluate the performance of our proposed HTRN model. Our results demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach and its potential for advancing the state-of-the-art in MMC.

\subsection{Hierarchical Transformer-Based Reasoning Network}
The hierarchical transformer-based reasoning network (HTRN) is a novel approach for multimodal machine comprehension that leverages the power of transformers and hierarchical reasoning. The HTRN model consists of three main components: a transformer-based encoder, a hierarchical reasoning module, and a transformer-based decoder. The transformer-based encoder is responsible for encoding the multimodal context (e.g. an image and a caption) into a sequence of hidden states. The hierarchical reasoning module then performs reasoning and inference over the encoded context to generate an answer. The transformer-based decoder is responsible for decoding the answer from the hidden states generated by the hierarchical reasoning module. The HTRN model is trained end-to-end using a combination of supervised and reinforcement learning techniques. In this paper, we propose a novel hierarchical transformer-based reasoning network (HTRN) for multimodal machine comprehension and evaluate its performance on the RecipeQA dataset. Our results demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach and its potential for advancing the state-of-the-art in MMC. <|section_end|>

 This paper studies continual learning (CL) of a sequence of natural language processing (NLP) tasks in the task continual learning (Task-CL) setting. It aims to (i) prevent catastrophic forgetting (CF), and (ii) transfer knowledge across tasks. (ii) is particularly important because many tasks in NLP share  a lot of common knowledge. For example, in machine translation (MT), the translation of a sentence in a language (e.g., English) can be used to infer the translation of a similar sentence in another language (e.g., Chinese). However, most existing CL methods focus only on overcoming CF and have no mechanism to encourage knowledge transfer (KT) across tasks. As a result, they do not do well in KT. For example, the popular method, elastic weight consolidation (EWC) \cite{arxivID-1712.03847-0}, does not explicitly encourage KT, and thus it does not do well in KT. Although there are several papers that try to deal with both CF and KT, our experiments show that they suffer from serious CF when the tasks do not have much shared knowledge. Another observation is that most current CL methods do not use pre-trained models, but it has been shown that such models can significantly improve the end task performance. For example, in natural language processing, fine-tuning a BERT-like pre-trained language model is one of the most effective approaches. However, for CL, this approach suffers from serious CF. An interesting question is how to make the best use of pre-trained models for CL.

This paper proposes a novel model called CTR to solve these problems. Our experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of CTR.
