The research of backdoor attack is mainly conducted on image data. In this work, we aim to fill the gap in the domain of point clouds, which is of great importance in many real-world applications. However, it is non-trivial to design a backdoor attack method for point clouds, as it has its own unique characteristics. For instance, point clouds are discrete and unordered data, and there are no pixels or pixels neighborhoods in point clouds, which makes it hard to implant triggers. Besides, existing backdoor attack methods are mainly designed for image data, easily defended by some point cloud specific operations (such as denoising). To address these challenges, we propose Poisoning MorphNet, a novel backdoor attack method for point clouds. Our contributions are summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}
\item Poisoning MorphNet is the first backdoor attack method on point clouds. It jointly optimizes two objectives for sample-adaptive poisoning: a reconstruction loss that preserves the visual similarity between benign / poisoned point clouds, and a classification loss that enforces a modern recognition model of point clouds tends to mis-classify the poisoned sample to a pre-specified target category. This implicitly conducts spectral separation over point clouds, hiding sample-adaptive triggers in fine-grained high-frequency details.
\item We propose a third loss in Poisoning MorphNet for suppressing isolated points, leading to improved resistance to denoising-based defense.
\item Comprehensive evaluations are conducted on ModelNet40 and ShapeNetcorev2. Our proposed Poisoning MorphNet outstrips all previous methods with clear margins.
