Definition generation tasks have been widely studied in recent years, and most of them are based on encoder-decoder models \cite{arxivID-1406.1078-5}. However, most of the models can only generate definitions with the same complexity level, which is not enough to meet the needs of different scenarios. For example, when teaching children, we need to use simple and easy-to-understand language to help them understand the meaning of words. In addition, the definitions of words in the dictionary are usually concise and abstract, which is not conducive to helping language learners understand the meaning of words. Therefore, it is necessary to generate definitions with different complexity levels to meet the needs of different scenarios.

To address these problems, we propose the task of generating definitions with different complexity levels. We collect a new dataset named \textbf{COMPILING} which contains definitions of different complexity levels. We first collect definitions from the Chinese dictionary and then use a complexity classifier to classify the complexity of the definitions. We use the definitions of different complexity levels to train the model, so that the model can generate definitions with different complexity levels. We also conduct experiments to evaluate the performance of the model on the dataset. The experimental results show that the model can generate definitions with different complexity levels.

The main contributions of this paper are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
