One of the most significant challenges in developing a socially-aware navigation system is the ability to predict the behavior of humans in the environment. Humans have complex and diverse behaviors, making it difficult to model their behavior and predict their movements. To address this challenge, we propose a socially-aware navigation system that learns from human demonstrations. Our system uses the human demonstration data to learn a navigation policy that considers social norms and rules, as well as the behavior of humans in the environment. By learning from human demonstrations, our system can better predict the behavior of humans and make more socially-aware decisions in crowded public spaces.

In this work, we propose a novel socially-aware navigation system that learns from human demonstrations using Trajectory-ranked Maximum Entropy Deep Inverse Reinforcement Learning (T-MEDIRL) \cite{arxivID-2209.07996-0}. To address the limitation of handcrafted features, we propose a multi-modal learning framework that incorporates a human trajectory prediction module as one of the feature layers. By using this approach, our system can learn a more comprehensive representation of the environment and make more informed decisions in crowded public spaces. Our contributions can be summarized as follows:
