\item We propose a multi-modal learning framework that incorporates a human trajectory prediction module as one of the feature layers. This approach allows our system to learn a more comprehensive representation of the environment and make more informed decisions in crowded public spaces.
\item We evaluate our system in a simulated environment and demonstrate its effectiveness in navigating crowded public spaces while considering social norms and rules.
\end{itemize}
Related Work
\label{sec:Related Work}
\subsection{Socially-Aware Navigation}
Socially-aware navigation is an important aspect of robot navigation, as robots must navigate in environments where humans are present. There are two main approaches to socially-aware navigation: model-based and model-free. Model-based methods use social models to predict human behavior and plan accordingly. Model-free methods use reinforcement learning to learn a navigation policy from experience. 

One of the most commonly used social models in socially-aware navigation is the social force model \cite{arxivID-2109.12081-1}. The social force model represents pedestrian behavior as a set of forces that push and pull pedestrians towards their goals and away from obstacles. This model has been widely used in socially-aware navigation because it provides a simple and effective way to model pedestrian behavior. Other social models that have been used in socially-aware navigation include the velocity obstacle (VO) model \cite{arxivID-2004.10976-2} and the social impact model \cite{arxivID-1810.06613-3}.

Model-free methods for socially-aware navigation use reinforcement learning to learn a navigation policy from experience. These methods have been shown to be effective in complex and dynamic environments, where it is difficult to model human behavior. One of the most commonly used reinforcement learning algorithms for socially-aware navigation is deep reinforcement learning (DRL) \cite{arxivID-2010.14838-4}. DRL methods have been used to learn navigation policies for both social navigation and non-social navigation. In social navigation, DRL methods have been used to learn policies that consider social norms and rules, such as maintaining a social distance away from humans \cite{arxivID-2010.14838-5}. In non-social navigation, DRL methods have been used to learn policies that avoid collisions with obstacles \cite{arxivID-2006.09792-6}. DRL methods have also been used to learn policies that are robust to changes in the environment, such as changes in the distribution of obstacles \cite{arxivID-2111.03915-7}. 

\subsection{Trajectory-Ranked Maximum Entropy Deep Inverse Reinforcement Learning}
The Trajectory-Ranked Maximum Entropy Deep Inverse Reinforcement Learning (T-MEDIRL) is a method for learning a reward function from expert demonstrations. T-MEDIRL is based on the principle of maximum entropy, which is a way of modeling uncertainty in a system. The maximum entropy principle states that the probability distribution that best represents the current state of knowledge is the one with the largest entropy. In the context of T-MEDIRL, this means that the reward function should be as uncertain as possible, while still being consistent with the expert demonstrations. 

T-MEDIRL has been used in a variety of applications, including robotics, autonomous driving, and computer vision. In robotics, T-MEDIRL has been used to learn reward functions for robot control tasks, such as locomotion and manipulation. In autonomous driving, T-MEDIRL has been used to learn reward functions for autonomous vehicle control tasks, such as lane changing and obstacle avoidance. In computer vision, T-MEDIRL has been used to learn reward functions for image recognition tasks, such as object detection and segmentation. 

In this paper, we propose a novel socially-aware navigation system that learns from human demonstrations using T-MEDIRL. The system can predict human behavior and make socially-aware decisions in crowded public spaces. We evaluate our system in a simulated environment and demonstrate its effectiveness in navigating crowded public spaces while considering social norms and rules. <|section_end|>

