ystem-level challenges, such as data movement between the IMC device and the digital accelerators, which can significantly impact the performance and energy efficiency of the overall system. Moreover, the design of a system-level solution that can effectively leverage the benefits of both IMC and digital accelerators requires a deep understanding of the interaction between these two technologies, as well as the specific requirements of the DNN workloads that will be executed on the system. In this work, we present a tightly-coupled heterogeneous architecture integrating a RISC-V cluster, a digital accelerator, and an in-memory computing accelerator (IMA). The RISC-V cluster is responsible for loading the DNN weights from the IMC device and executing the most computationally intensive layers of the DNN, while the digital accelerator is used for the execution of the remaining layers. The IMA is used for the execution of the first layers of the DNN, which are typically less computationally intensive and can be efficiently accelerated using IMC. The proposed architecture is evaluated on a highly heterogeneous workload, such as the Bottleneck layer of a MobileNetV2, showing that the integration of IMC with digital accelerators can provide significant improvements in terms of performance and energy efficiency. Furthermore, we explore the requirements for end-to-end inference of a full mobile-grade DNN (MobileNetV2) in terms of IMC array resources, by scaling up our heterogeneous architecture to a multi-array accelerator. Our results show that our solution, on the end-to-end inference of the MobileNetV2, is one order of magnitude better in terms of execution latency than existing programmable architectures and two orders of magnitude better than state-of-the-art heterogeneous solutions integrating in-memory computing analog cores.

