 Most software operates in environments characterised by workloads, usage profiles, failures and available resources that are stochastic in nature #ref9# #ref20# #ref40#. As such, probabilistic models such as Markov chains #ref27# #ref31# #ref44#, queueing networks #ref6# #ref28# and stochastic Petri nets #ref7# #ref43# #ref45# have long been used to analyse the performability (i.e., the performance, dependability and other nonfunctional properties) of software. In this paper, we focus on the analysis of software performability using parametric discrete-time Markov chains (pDTMCs), i.e., Markov chains that  contain unknown parameters, which are typically probabilities, transition rates or sojourn times. The analysis of pDTMCs aims to compute the value of the unknown parameters that maximises or minimises a given performance or dependability measure, such as the average response time, the availability, the throughput or the reliability of the system. The problem of computing the optimal value of the unknown parameters is known as the \emph{parametric model checking (PMC)} problem. PMC has been widely used in the formal analysis of probabilistic models of computer systems and communication protocols, and has been extensively studied in the literature, e.g., see \cite{arxivID-2207.06801-0} for a survey. However, the analysis of software performability using pDTMCs has not received much attention in the literature.

The aim of this paper is to introduce a new PMC technique, called \emph{fast parametric model checking (fPMC)}, that can be used to analyse software performability properties of software systems modelled by pDTMCs. The fPMC technique works by automatically decomposing a pDTMC model of the software system under verification into fragments that can be analysed independently, yielding results that are then combined to establish the required software performability properties. The new PMC technique is based on the notion of \emph{parametric reachability}, i.e., the analysis of the reachability properties of a pDTMC, where the unknown parameters are treated as variables that can take any value from a given parameter domain. The fPMC technique is efficient and can handle software systems modelled by pDTMCs that are too complex to be handled by existing PMC methods. Furthermore, for many pDTMCs that state-of-the-art parametric model checkers can analyse, fPMC produces solutions (i.e., algebraic formulae) that are simpler and much faster to evaluate. We show experimentally that adding fPMC to the existing repertoire of PMC methods improves the efficiency of parametric model checking significantly, and extends its applicability to software systems with more complex behaviour than currently possible.

\subsection{Related work}

The analysis of software performability properties using probabilistic models has been widely studied in the literature, e.g., see \cite{arxivID-1606.06877-1}. The analysis of software performability using pDTMCs has not received much attention in the literature, however. We are only aware of two papers that consider the analysis of software performability properties using pDTMCs. The first paper \cite{arxivID-1805.09614-2} proposes a method for the automatic refinement of a pDTMC model of a software system to a more accurate model. The refined model is then used to establish software performability properties that were not derivable from the original model. The second paper \cite{arxivID-1405.0327-3} proposes a method for the analysis of software performability properties using pDTMCs that are too complex to be handled by existing parametric model checkers. The method works by first decomposing a pDTMC model of the software system under verification into a set of fragments that can be analysed independently, and then by combining the results obtained from the fragments to establish the required software performability properties. The method is based on the notion of \emph{parametric reachability}, i.e., the analysis of the reachability properties of a pDTMC, where the unknown parameters are treated as variables that can take any value from a given parameter domain. In contrast to the method presented in the second paper, fPMC can handle software systems modelled by pDTMCs that are too complex to be handled by existing PMC methods. Furthermore, for many pDTMCs that state-of-the-art parametric model checkers can analyse, fPMC produces solutions (i.e., algebraic formulae) that are simpler and much faster to evaluate.

\subsection{Contributions}

The main contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We introduce a new PMC technique, called \emph{fast parametric model checking (fPMC)}, that can be used to analyse software performability properties of software systems modelled by pDTMCs.
\item We show experimentally that adding fPMC to the existing repertoire of PMC methods improves the efficiency of parametric model checking significantly, and extends its applicability to software systems with more complex behaviour than currently possible.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this paper is structured as follows. Section~\ref{sec:preliminaries} introduces the notation and background. Section~\ref{sec:proposed} presents the fPMC technique. Section~\ref{sec:implementation} describes the implementation of fPMC. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} presents experimental results. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper. <|section_end|>

