 In recent years, much interest has been given to the modeling and/or simulation of tethered systems, where the dynamics of interconnected bodies is studied (see, e.g. #ref26# #ref41# #ref24# #ref25# #ref43# #ref44# #ref40# #ref42# #ref2# #ref32# #ref33#). It turns out that the underlying dynamics is often described by  a Hamiltonian system subject to holonomic constraints, which are of quadratic type. In this paper, we consider a class of Hamiltonian systems with quadratic holonomic constraints, which read as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:constr}
\mathbf{q}^\top\mathbf{B}\mathbf{q}=0,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ is a symmetric matrix, which is positive definite, and $\mathbf{q}\in\mathbb{R}^n$ is the vector of the so-called ``internal coordinates'' of the system, which are not necessarily all the degrees of freedom. To be more precise, let us denote by $\mathbf{q}=(q_1,\ldots,q_n)^\top$ and by $\mathbf{Q}=(Q_1,\ldots,Q_d)^\top$, with $d\ge n$, the vectors of all the coordinates of the system. Then, $\mathbf{q}$ is defined as the vector of coordinates such that $\mathbf{Q}$ can be expressed as a function of $\mathbf{q}$, namely
\begin{equation}\label{eq:q}
\mathbf{Q}=\mathbf{Q}(\mathbf{q}).
\end{equation}
Let us now introduce the Hamiltonian function
\begin{equation}\label{eq:H}
H(\mathbf{p},\mathbf{q})=\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{p}^\top\mathbf{M}\mathbf{p}+V(\mathbf{q}),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{p}\in\mathbb{R}^n$ is the vector of the conjugate momenta, $\mathbf{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ is a symmetric matrix, which is positive definite, and $V(\mathbf{q})$ is the potential energy. Then, the dynamics of the system is described by the Hamiltonian system
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Ham}
\begin{cases}
\dot{\mathbf{q}}=\frac{\partial H}{\partial\mathbf{p}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{p},\\
\dot{\mathbf{p}}=-\frac{\partial H}{\partial\mathbf{q}}=-\nabla V(\mathbf{q}),
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
which is subject to the holonomic constraint \eqref{eq:constr}.

In this paper, we will focus on the numerical solution of \eqref{eq:Ham}--\eqref{eq:constr}, so as to preserve the quadratic constraint \eqref{eq:constr}. In the following, we will refer to the numerical methods that are able to do so as ``energy-conserving methods'', since the constraint \eqref{eq:constr} implies that the Hamiltonian function $H$ is constant along the solution of the constrained system \eqref{eq:Ham}--\eqref{eq:constr}.

The problem of constructing energy-conserving methods for Hamiltonian systems subject to holonomic constraints is not new. In fact, it has been addressed in a number of papers, both in the case of linear and quadratic holonomic constraints. In particular, the case of linear constraints has been studied in \cite{arxivID-2306.06786-0}, where the authors propose a general framework to construct energy-conserving methods for Hamiltonian systems subject to linear constraints. In the same paper, the authors also show that the so-called Hamiltonian Boundary Value Methods (HBVMs) \cite{arxivID-2203.03095-1, arxivID-2203.11189-2}, which are a class of energy-conserving methods for Hamiltonian systems, are shown to be a particular case of the framework proposed in \cite{arxivID-2306.06786-3}. The case of linear constraints has also been studied in \cite{arxivID-1807.00794-4}, where the authors propose a general framework to construct energy-conserving methods for Hamiltonian systems subject to linear constraints, and in \cite{arxivID-1604.01261-5}, where the authors consider the problem of solving linear-quadratic optimal control problems subject to linear constraints.

